> Predict signed noise,and subtract it

For a noisy observation y=x+v,the network predicts r_theta(y),and its clean
estimate is y-r_theta(y). The source objective is
\(L=\tfrac12\operatorname{mean}(r-v)^2\),so each noise-output derivative is
(r-v)/numel. The clean shortcut has derivatives+1 with respect to y and-1
with respect to predicted r. Neither input nor output is clamped during fitting.
Clipping appears only in the declared display/clipped-PSNR comparison.
```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dncnn_residual.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const auto r = n::arange(-.5, .5001, .01, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  const auto target = n::full(r.shape(), .2, n::DType::Float64);
  const auto loss = m::DnResidualLoss(r, target),
             gradient = n::grad(loss, {r})[0] * double(r.numel());
  auto a = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), b = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  a.lines(r.to_vector<double>(), (.5 * n::square(r - .2)).to_vector<double>(),
          "half squared residual", {37, 99, 235})
      .title("A signed noise-regression objective")
      .x_label("predicted residual r;target .2")
      .y_label("per-sample half squared error")
      .y_limits(0, .3);
  b.lines(r.to_vector<double>(), gradient.to_vector<double>(), "local derivative",
          {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Residual gradient is r minus target")
      .x_label("predicted residual r")
      .y_label("unaveraged derivative")
      .y_limits(-.8, .5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(a).add(b).size(1100, 480).fit_panels().save_svg("build/dncnn_residual.svg");
  const auto result = m::DnSubtract(n::tensor({-.2, 1.2}), n::tensor({.1, -.2}));
  std::print(
      "Unclipped signed subtraction: {:.3f},{:.3f};no activation at the clean output.\n",
      result.to_vector<double>()[0], result.to_vector<double>()[1]);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Unclipped signed subtraction: -0.300,1.400;no activation at the clean output.
\image(figures/dncnn_residual.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Half-MSE and every residual-output derivative. The curve’s minimum is the signed target noise;the clean prediction subtracts this quantity rather than applying a final sigmoid.)
\alttext(Half-MSE and every residual-output derivative. The curve’s minimum is the signed target noise;the clean prediction subtracts this quantity rather than applying a final sigmoid.)

> A reflected mean-filter reference

A fixed3x3 mean has no fitted parameters. At a boundary,it reflects sample
indices without duplicating the edge. On the2x3 array[[0,1,2],[3,4,5]],the
upper-left3x3 neighborhood averages to8/3. The full result is
[[8/3,3,10/3],[5/3,2,7/3]]. Singleton axes are supported too. Native oracles
check every reflected value and every input derivative on several geometries.
```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dncnn_filter.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const auto x = n::tensor({0., 1., 2., 3., 4., 5.}, {1, 2, 3, 1});
  const auto y = m::DnBoxFilter(x);
  auto a = n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(x.reshape({2, 3}), {0}), 0, 5),
       b = n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(y.reshape({2, 3}), {0}), 0, 5);
  a.title("Six original intensities").x_label("column").y_label("original row0 at bottom");
  b.title("Reflected3x3 mean").x_label("column").y_label("original row0 at bottom");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(a).add(b).size(1050, 500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/dncnn_filter.svg");
  std::print("Reflected upper-left mean {:.6f};reflection maps index-1 on length3 to {}.\n",
             y.to_vector<double>()[0], m::DnReflect(-1, 3));
}
```
